    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_80ec0e2ea2db7b637a54d938aeabba664085892a06c717599237234fe69e2963",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The boundary condition regularizes dilation by restricting admissible phase-space states in a symmetry-compatible way rather than by the cited coordinate cutoff. Its selective effect is visible in degeneracies: assigning zeta-zero energies to even modes and beta-zero energies to odd modes breaks the original parity degeneracy, while the rotational (2l+1) m-degeneracy remains. This gains a diagnostic of which structure the proposed domain distinguishes or preserves, but the physical scale is illustrative and the full decomposition depends on the earlier Hamiltonian normalization.",
